{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgbjLiMZZZQJjZVBYoaK7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I didn't compute a melting point for n-eicosane with MACE-MPA-0. The model failed the validation test on this very material, so any number it gave would have an error of unknown size. I recorded that as a refusal on the board.\n\n**Premise check.** The melting point itself is well defined: the measured value is 310 ± 1 K (NIST WebBook, from `tier1.json`). The problem is the model. MACE-MPA-0 was trained on inorganic crystal data with no dispersion correction and almost no molecular organic crystals. Paraffin chains are held together by van der Waals forces, which that training data doesn't cover.\n\n**Validation.** I relaxed a 124-atom model crystal of two eicosane molecules with MACE-MPA-0 (`scripts/crystal_check.py`):\n- Starting from a standard alkane packing at 0.922 g/cm³, the model pushed outward with stresses of −2.3 to −4.4 GPa, meaning the cell wanted to expand strongly.\n- After 1500 steps the relaxation still hadn't converged and was still expanding. It had already reached 0.764 g/cm³.\n- That is at least 11% below the measured solid density of 0.856 g/cm³ in `tier1.json`. A fully relaxed (0 K) crystal should be denser than the room-temperature measurement, not lighter, so the model fails to reproduce a known result for this material.\n- I'm not relying on the binding energies (−103 and −206 kJ/mol per molecule), because the isolated-molecule reference was only loosely relaxed.\n- I also ran a scan that squeezed and stretched the cell sideways, but it distorted the molecules themselves, so I discarded it.\n\n**Cost of a proper run.** Even with a suitable model, a credible melting point needs a box with solid and liquid side by side. Simply heating the crystal overshoots the melting point by tens of K. That box would be about 10,000 atoms, run for several ns at each temperature. Eicosane also has a solid-solid transition at about 305 K, just below melting. Running all of that on an under-bound model would give a confident-looking number I can't put a meaningful uncertainty on.\n\n**Proposed alternative.** Use a model that includes dispersion: MACE-OFF24, or MACE-MPA-0 with a D3(BJ) correction.\n1. Check it first against the 0.856 g/cm³ density, and against the measured melting points of n-hexadecane (291 K) and n-octadecane (301 K) in `tier1.json`.\n2. Then run solid–liquid coexistence simulations between about 290 and 330 K.\n3. Report the temperature range that brackets melting, plus the model's error on the two calibration alkanes, as the uncertainty.\n\n**Board:**\n- candidate `ea5b3ba3…` (n-eicosane), which fulfils need #0 of the request.\n- run_report `7770319f…` (status `refused`), with the full reasoning and validation numbers.\n\nNo `property_result` was posted."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2198,"cache_read_input_tokens":40639,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2198,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2329f7f8-7fe6-4b53-88fa-9ab17cb3f3cb","uuid":"0514b331-ddde-40ea-92e3-d8ed1210d186","timestamp":"2026-11-01T06:25:28.903Z","request_id":"req_011CgbjLhzj1CMrAva3jbXQi"}
